direction: right

title: |md
  ## fexit vs kretprobe — same goal, very different mechanics
| {near: top-center; shape: text}

kretprobe: "kretprobe (legacy)" {
  style.fill: "#fee2e2"
  body_kp: |md
    1. At entry: kprobe fires, save real return address
    2. **Overwrite return address on stack** with trampoline
    3. Function runs, returns to trampoline
    4. Trampoline calls your kretprobe handler
    5. Handler reads `pt_regs->ax` for return value
    6. Trampoline jumps to real return address

    **Costs:**
    - int3 trap on entry (~50 ns)
    - Limited "instances pool" — bursts can miss returns
    - No access to original arguments
    - Stack-modifying tricks break C++ unwinding
  |
}

fexit: "fexit (modern)" {
  style.fill: "#dcfce7"
  body_fx: |md
    1. At entry: trampoline saves args
    2. Calls fentry programs (if any)
    3. Calls original function
    4. **Trampoline appends ret value to ctx**
    5. Calls fexit programs with full ctx (args + ret)
    6. Returns to caller

    **Costs:**
    - jmp on entry (~10 ns)
    - No pool, no missed returns
    - Original arguments **and** return value typed
    - Stack untouched — unwinding safe
  |
}

verdict: "Use fexit when available\n(only fall back to kretprobe for non-BTF functions)" {
  style.fill: "#fef3c7"
}

kretprobe -> verdict
fexit -> verdict
